requirement:
11.1.     EXEC-001 Execution Eligibility

A pricing action may enter execution only after the Pricing Initiative has satisfied the
execution-readiness gate defined in FLOW-030 and has entered Ready for Execution.

Execution must be bound to the exact controlled Pricing Proposal version that provides the
current execution authority under PROP-021.

The applicable Proposal version must:

    ● have Pricing Proposal status Approved or Authorised Within Policy;
    ● remain the Current applicable Proposal version and valid for execution;
    ● be identifiable as the exact authorised decision baseline;
    ● retain the applicable decision snapshot;
    ● have a defined pricing scope and executable pricing action;
    ● satisfy applicable effective timing and execution conditions.

Before execution begins, the system must also verify that:

    ● the acting user possesses the required execution authority;
    ● no blocking workflow condition remains;
    ● no blocking governance condition remains;
    ● required implementation information is available for the applicable pricing action.

Failure of any required execution condition must prevent execution from beginning or
continuing until the condition is appropriately resolved.

A Draft, Awaiting Approval, Changes Requested, Rejected, Superseded or Expired Proposal
version must not provide execution authority.

The execution layer must not independently approve, policy-authorise, modify or reinterpret
the Pricing Proposal that provides its execution authority.

11.2.    EXEC-002 Ready for Execution State

When all execution conditions are satisfied, the Pricing Initiative must enter Ready for
Execution.

The applicable Pricing Proposal must retain its Approved or Authorised Within Policy status
and remain the exact controlled Proposal version providing execution authority.

The system must clearly display:

   ● authorised pricing action;
   ● applicable Pricing Proposal status;
   ● authorised Pricing Proposal version;
   ● affected pricing scope;
   ● authorised prices, pricing rule or pricing logic, as applicable;
   ● effective date or effective period;
   ● implementation owner;
   ● relevant implementation instructions;
   ● conditions attached to the applicable approval or policy authorisation.

Ready for Execution indicates that the Pricing Initiative is permitted to proceed into
implementation.

It must not indicate that implementation has already occurred, modify the Pricing Proposal's
decision status or create a new source of execution authority.

11.3.    EXEC-003 Execution Ownership

An authorised user must be assignable as the implementation owner.

Execution ownership determines responsibility for carrying out, coordinating or recording
implementation of the authorised pricing action.

Execution ownership does not itself grant authority to:

   ● modify the authorised Pricing Proposal version;
   ● change the authorised pricing action;
   ● change the authorised pricing scope;
   ● alter the decision snapshot;
   ● create approval or policy-authorisation authority.

Any material difference between the authorised decision baseline and the action intended for
implementation must follow the applicable execution-variance and governance requirements.

11.4.     EXEC-004 Execution Instructions

The authorised pricing action must provide enough structured information for the
implementation owner to determine exactly what must be implemented.

Where applicable, execution instructions should identify:

   ● affected priceable entity or pricing scope;
   ● applicable location, channel, customer group or other pricing dimension;
   ● relevant current or baseline pricing state;
   ● authorised price, pricing value, pricing rule or pricing logic;
   ● effective date or effective period;
   ● temporary pricing period where applicable;
   ● authorised exceptions;
   ● implementation conditions;
   ● relevant operational instructions.

Execution instructions must derive from the exact controlled Pricing Proposal version
providing execution authority.

The execution layer must not silently substitute later workspace values, another Scenario,
another Proposal version or changed pricing logic for the authorised execution baseline.

11.5.     EXEC-005 Manual External Execution

Where the actual price change occurs in an external system, authorised users must be able
to record the implementation inside Owómidé.

External implementation may occur through systems such as:

   ● ERP;
   ● POS;
   ● marketplace;
   ● commerce platform;
   ● internal business system;
   ● another operational pricing channel.

Owómidé must not represent external execution as completed until an authorised user
records or confirms it.

11.6.   EXEC-006 Implementation Confirmation

An authorised Executor must be able to confirm that the pricing action has been
implemented.

Confirmation must capture:

   ● implementing user;
   ● implementation timestamp;
   ● actual effective date;
   ● implementation status;
   ● actual implemented pricing state where applicable.

11.8.    EXEC-008 Execution Variance

The system must identify where actual implementation differs from the authorised pricing
action or execution baseline.

Relevant variance may include:

   ● different implemented price or pricing value;
   ● different pricing rule or logic;
   ● different affected scope;
   ● different location, channel or other pricing dimension;
   ● different effective date or period;
   ● omitted entities;
   ● additional entities;
   ● different promotional or temporary pricing period;
   ● unauthorised exception or condition.

Execution variance must remain visible within the Pricing Initiative and linked to the exact
Pricing Proposal version that provided execution authority.

The existence of a variance must not modify the original authorised Proposal version or
decision record.

Material execution variance must be treated according to EXEC-009.

11.11.       EXEC-011 Failed or Blocked Implementation

Where implementation cannot be completed, the Executor must be able to record:

   ● affected scope;
   ● failure or blocking reason;
   ● timestamp;
   ● relevant notes.

The initiative must remain unresolved until the execution state is appropriately addressed.

11.12.       EXEC-012 Implementation Evidence

Users should be able to attach relevant evidence confirming execution.

Examples include:

   ● system export;

   ● screenshot;
   ● implementation report;
   ● internal confirmation;
   ● other supporting evidence.

Evidence must remain attached to the execution record.

11.13.       EXEC-013 Execution History

Consequential execution events must be recorded in chronological history.

This includes:

   ● assignment;
   ● readiness for execution;
   ● implementation started;
   ● partial implementation;
   ● implementation confirmation;
   ● implementation failure;
   ● implementation variance;
   ● correction.

11.16.        EXEC-016 Monitoring Period

Where relevant, users should be able to define a monitoring period or review date.

This may be based on:

    ● fixed date;
    ● number of days after implementation;
    ● promotional period;
    ● organisation-defined review point.

The MVP does not require continuous automated monitoring.

11.17.        EXEC-017 Monitoring Metrics

Users must be able to record relevant post-implementation outcomes where information is
available.

Supported outcome categories may include:

    ● sales;
    ● revenue;
    ● volume;
    ● gross margin;
    ● gross profit;
    ● customer response;

   ● commercial feedback;
   ● operational feedback;
   ● pricing exceptions.

11.19.     EXEC-019 Objective Assessment

Users should be able to record whether the initiative's original pricing objective was:

   ● Achieved;
   ● Partially Achieved;
   ● Not Achieved;
   ● Unable to Determine.

The assessment may include explanatory notes.

11.20.     EXEC-020 Qualitative Outcome Recording

Monitoring must not require every pricing outcome to be expressed numerically.

Users must be able to record qualitative outcomes such as:

   ● customer resistance;
   ● improved competitiveness;
   ● distributor feedback;
   ● sales-team feedback;
   ● unexpected operational consequences;
   ● competitor response.

11.22.     EXEC-022 AI Monitoring Assistance

AI may assist users in interpreting recorded post-implementation information.

AI may:

   ● summarise outcomes;
   ● compare outcomes with the initiative objective;
   ● identify notable changes;
   ● surface possible lessons;
   ● help prepare a closing summary.

AI must not fabricate missing performance data or claim causation that the available
evidence does not support.

11.24.     EXEC-024 Follow-Up Pricing Work

Where monitoring indicates that further pricing action is required, an authorised user should
be able to create a new Pricing Initiative using relevant context from the completed initiative.

The new initiative must remain a separate pricing record with its own:

   ● objective;
   ● versions;

   ● organisational decision records;
   ● execution history.

11.25.     EXEC-025 Completion Readiness

An implemented initiative may be completed when its required operational obligations are
resolved.

Depending on the initiative, completion may require:

   ● implementation finished;
   ● execution exceptions resolved;
   ● required monitoring information recorded;
   ● objective assessment completed.

Not every initiative must require an extended monitoring period before completion.

11.26.     EXEC-026 Completion Summary

A completed Pricing Initiative should preserve a concise final summary sufficient to connect
the original pricing objective with the organisational decision, actual implementation and
recorded outcome.

The summary should contain, where applicable:

   ● original pricing objective;
   ● exact Pricing Proposal version that provided execution authority;
   ● whether that Proposal version was Approved or Authorised Within Policy;
   ● authorised pricing action;
   ● actual implemented pricing action or state;
   ● significant execution variances or Implementation Exceptions;
   ● relevant monitored outcomes;
   ● final objective assessment;
   ● final Pricing Initiative status.

The completion summary must distinguish what was authorised from what was actually
implemented.

It must not overwrite or simplify away material differences preserved in the Proposal,
execution or monitoring records.

11.29.     EXEC-029 Execution Auditability

For every consequential implemented pricing action, Owómidé must preserve enough
information to determine:

    ● which exact Pricing Proposal version provided execution authority;
    ● whether that Proposal version was Approved or Authorised Within Policy;
    ● what exact pricing action and scope were authorised;
    ● who was responsible for implementation;
    ● what was actually implemented;
    ● when implementation occurred;
    ● whether implementation matched the authorised decision baseline;
    ● what execution variance or Implementation Exception occurred, where applicable;
    ● what happened after implementation.

Execution auditability must preserve the distinction between organisational decision authority
and actual implementation.

A later Pricing Proposal, pricing change or workspace update must not rewrite the execution
basis or actual implementation record of the earlier action.

12.1.     HIST-001 Persistent Pricing Record

Every Pricing Initiative must maintain a persistent historical record containing the relevant:

   ● objective;
   ● pricing scope;
   ● pricing context;
   ● assumptions;
   ● pricing logic;
   ● analysis;
   ● scenarios;
   ● proposals;
   ● collaboration;
   ● reviews;
   ● organisational decision records, including approvals and policy authorisations;
   ● execution;
   ● monitoring;
   ● lifecycle events.

Closing or completing an initiative must not remove this record.

12.5.     HIST-005 Previous Version Preservation

Creating a new version must not overwrite the previous version.

Users with appropriate access must be able to retrieve previous versions and determine:

   ● what that version contained;
   ● when it was created;
   ● who created or submitted it;
   ● what decision was made against it.

12.6.     HIST-006 Version Comparison

The MVP should allow users to compare relevant controlled versions of pricing work.

Comparison should identify material changes such as:

   ● price changes;

    ● scope changes;
    ● assumption changes;
    ● pricing-rule changes;
    ● analysis changes;
    ● guardrail-state changes.

Users should not have to manually compare historical records to determine what changed
between submissions.

12.8.     HIST-008 Historical Preservation of Proposal Decision Snapshot

For every submitted Pricing Proposal version, the system must preserve the complete
controlled decision snapshot established under PROP-011 as part of the Pricing Initiative's
historical record.

The historical record must preserve the Proposal snapshot exactly as it existed when that
version was submitted, including its relationships to the underlying pricing context, analysis,
assumptions, evidence, guardrail evaluation and other decision-relevant source objects.

Subsequent changes to:

    ● current prices;

   ● costs or other financial inputs;
   ● pricing context;
   ● Pricing Builder logic;
   ● assumptions;
   ● analysis;
   ● evidence;
   ● guardrails;
   ● workflow or governance configuration;
   ● other workspace data

must not modify, recalculate or reinterpret the historical Proposal decision snapshot.

Where a referenced source object changes after submission, the historical Proposal version
must continue to resolve to the version or value that formed part of its original decision
snapshot.

Historical preservation must not independently redefine the contents of the Proposal
decision snapshot. The canonical snapshot contract is governed by PROP-011.

12.9.     HIST-009 Activity Timeline

Every Pricing Initiative must contain a chronological activity timeline.

Relevant events should include:

   ● initiative creation;
   ● scope changes;
   ● significant edits;
   ● comments;
   ● assignments;
   ● evidence additions;
   ● scenario creation;
   ● review submission;
   ● proposal submission;
   ● requested changes;
   ● approvals;
   ● policy authorisations;
   ● rejections;
   ● escalation;

   ● execution events;
   ● monitoring updates;
   ● completion;
   ● cancellation.

12.10.      HIST-010 Activity Attribution

Each consequential activity event must identify:

   ● actor;
   ● action;
   ● timestamp;
   ● affected object;
   ● relevant version where applicable.

System-generated events must be distinguishable from human actions.

12.11.      HIST-011 AI Attribution

Where AI-assisted content materially affects pricing work, history must preserve:

   ● that AI assistance was involved;
   ● which user applied the output;
   ● when the change occurred;
   ● which pricing object was affected.

AI must not appear as the accountable human decision-maker.

12.12.      HIST-012 Administrative History

Consequential administrative changes affecting pricing governance must remain historically
available.

Examples include:

   ● role changes;
   ● permission changes;
   ● approval-authority changes;
   ● workflow changes;
   ● guardrail changes;

    ● cost-access changes;
    ● policy-authorisation configuration changes.

12.16.       HIST-016 Searchable Pricing History

Authorised users must be able to search historical pricing work.

Search should support relevant criteria such as:

    ● Pricing Initiative;
    ● product or SKU;
    ● category;
    ● owner;
    ● initiative type;
    ● status;
    ● decision date;
    ● implementation date;
    ● proposal outcome.

Search results must respect user permissions and scope.

12.17.     HIST-017 Historical Filtering

Users should be able to filter pricing history by relevant dimensions including:

   ● completed;
   ● rejected;
   ● cancelled;
   ● product or category;
   ● date range;
   ● location;
   ● channel;
   ● initiative type;
   ● responsible user.

12.18.     HIST-018 Historical Initiative View

A historical Pricing Initiative must remain understandable as a coherent record.

Users should be able to determine:

   ● why the initiative existed;
   ● what was considered;
   ● what was proposed;
   ● what changed;
   ● what organisational decision was made, including whether the applicable Pricing
       Proposal was Approved or Authorised Within Policy;
   ● what was implemented;
   ● what outcome was recorded.

12.19.     HIST-019 Organisational Pricing Knowledge

Completed pricing work should become reusable organisational knowledge.

Relevant previous work may provide context for:

   ● future Pricing Initiatives;
   ● pricing strategies;
   ● assumptions;

    ● scenarios;
    ● decision rationale;
    ● recurring pricing situations.

12.20.     HIST-020 Reuse Without Authority Transfer

Users may reuse appropriate structures or context from historical pricing work.

Reused content must create new working material.

Historical:

    ● Approval records;
    ● Policy Authorisation records;
    ● execution status;
    ● proposal validity

must not transfer to new pricing work.

12.21.     HIST-021 AI Historical Retrieval

AI may help authorised users locate and summarise relevant historical pricing work.

AI retrieval must respect:

    ● organisation boundaries;
    ● user access scope;
    ● sensitive-data permissions;
    ● historical context.

AI must distinguish historical decisions from current pricing instructions.

12.22.     HIST-022 Decision Rationale Preservation

The system must preserve enough structured and contextual information to explain why a
consequential pricing decision was made.

Rationale may include:

    ● stated objective;
    ● assumptions;

       ● supporting evidence;
       ● analysis;
       ● stakeholder input;
       ● proposal explanation;
       ● Approval comments or Policy Authorisation evaluation information.

A final price alone is not a sufficient historical record.

12.24.         HIST-024 Exportable Historical Record

Authorised users should be able to export relevant historical pricing records where needed
for:

       ● audit;
       ● management review;
       ● internal reporting;
       ● operational reference.

Exports must respect data-access restrictions.

12.25.         HIST-025 Audit Search

Users with appropriate audit authority should be able to inspect activity by relevant
dimensions such as:

       ● user;
       ● action type;
       ● Pricing Initiative;
       ● proposal;
       ● date range.

14.3.     EXPORT-003 Execution Export Content

Where applicable, an execution-oriented export should include:

    ● Pricing Initiative identifier;
    ● proposal identifier and version;
    ● Priceable Entity identifier;
    ● Priceable Entity name;
    ● Priceable Entity type;
    ● applicable category;
    ● location;
    ● channel;
    ● other relevant pricing dimensions;
    ● Current Price or other pricing baseline preserved in the exact authorised Proposal
        version's decision snapshot, where applicable and permitted;
    ● authorised price, pricing value, pricing rule or pricing logic, as applicable;
    ● currency;
    ● effective date;
    ● authorised exceptions;
    ● implementation notes where applicable.

The output must contain enough information for the receiving operational team to identify
what should change.

For execution-oriented exports affecting multiple Priceable Entities, each exported pricing
instruction must remain attributable to the applicable Priceable Entity and relevant pricing
dimensions.

A category, product group or other grouping selector must not replace the Priceable Entity
identifier required for operational implementation unless the receiving operational system
explicitly implements pricing at that same directly priceable object level.

14.5.     EXPORT-005 External Identifiers

Where an organisation maintains external identifiers such as:

   ● ERP product code;
   ● SKU;
   ● POS identifier;
   ● marketplace listing identifier;
   ● internal reference code,

those identifiers should be available in operational exports where configured.

This reduces manual mapping during implementation.

14.6.     EXPORT-006 Export Permission

Only users with appropriate export permissions may export protected pricing information.

Export authority must respect:

   ● organisation membership;
   ● role;
   ● resource;
   ● pricing scope;
   ● sensitive-data permissions.

14.8.     EXPORT-008 Export Type Separation

The system must distinguish between exports created for different purposes.

Relevant categories may include:

    ● pricing-context export;
    ● analysis export;
    ● proposal export;
    ● authorised-action execution export;
    ● historical or audit export.

An exploratory Scenario export must not be represented as an authorised execution
instruction.

14.9.     EXPORT-009 Proposal Status, Validity and Execution Status Visibility

Where an export contains Pricing Proposal or execution information, the system must
preserve the distinction between:

    ● Pricing Proposal Status;
    ● Pricing Proposal Version Validity;
    ● Execution Status.

Where Pricing Proposal Status is included, the exported value must use the canonical
Proposal statuses defined in PROP-012:

    ● Draft;
    ● Awaiting Approval;
    ● Changes Requested;
    ● Rejected;
    ● Approved;
    ● Authorised Within Policy.

Where Pricing Proposal Version Validity is included, the exported value must use the
canonical validity states defined in PROP-013:

    ● Current;
    ● Superseded;
    ● Expired.

Proposal Version Validity must not be merged into or represented as Pricing Proposal
Status.

Where execution information is included, it must be represented separately using the
applicable execution states defined in EXEC-010:

    ● Not Started;
    ● In Progress;
    ● Partially Implemented;
    ● Implemented;
    ● Implementation Exception.

An export must not represent a Pricing Proposal as Implemented because implementation is
an operational execution state rather than a Pricing Proposal status.

An Approved or Authorised Within Policy Proposal version that is Superseded or Expired
must preserve both pieces of information rather than replacing its historical Proposal status
with its current validity state.

Exported status information must remain consistent with the authoritative Proposal and
execution records inside Owómidé.

14.13.     EXPORT-013 Export Attribution

Consequential exports must be attributable to the user who generated them.

The system must preserve:

   ● exporting user;
   ● timestamp;
   ● export type;
   ● related Pricing Initiative;
   ● proposal version where applicable.

14.18.     EXPORT-018 Human-Readable Handoff

In addition to structured data, the MVP should provide an understandable summary of the
authorised action for the person responsible for implementation.

The handoff should make clear:

   ● what must change;
   ● where it applies;
   ● when it becomes effective;
   ● which version was authorised;
   ● any relevant exceptions or conditions.

14.19.     EXPORT-019 External Operational Handoff State

Where an authorised pricing action is handed to an external implementation process, the
system should be able to record that the action has been Exported or Handed Off for
Execution.

This state must identify:

   ● handoff timestamp;
   ● responsible user;
   ● affected proposal version;
   ● implementation owner where applicable.

14.21.      EXPORT-021 Implementation Reference

When implementation occurs outside Owómidé, authorised users should be able to record
an external implementation reference where available.

Examples include:

    ● ERP change reference;
    ● internal ticket number;
    ● batch identifier;
    ● marketplace update reference.

The MVP must not require an external reference where the organisation does not use one.

14.24.      EXPORT-024 Data Formatting

Exports must use consistent:

   ● column definitions;
   ● currencies;
   ● numeric formats;
   ● dates;
   ● identifiers.

The system must avoid ambiguous representations that could cause operational pricing
errors.

14.26.     EXPORT-026 Export Validation

Before generating an execution-oriented export, the system must validate that:

   ● the proposal has valid execution authority;
   ● the proposal version is current and identifiable;
   ● required implementation fields are present;
   ● affected scope is defined;
   ● applicable effective dates are valid;
   ● no blocking execution condition remains.

14.27.     EXPORT-027 Historical Export

Authorised users must be able to export relevant historical pricing records for legitimate
purposes such as:

   ● internal review;
   ● management reporting;
   ● audit;
   ● operational reference.

Historical exports must preserve original decision context rather than recalculate using
current workspace values.
